1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,625
Jurong Island: Seven Islands into One

2
00:00:03,625 --> 00:00:07,254
Almost every Singaporean
depends on Jurong Island,

3
00:00:07,334 --> 00:00:10,375
and almost none will ever set foot on it.

4
00:00:10,375 --> 00:00:14,398
The petrol in the car, the gas
that fires the power stations,

5
00:00:14,478 --> 00:00:18,771
the plastic in half the things at
home — all of it passes through

6
00:00:18,851 --> 00:00:23,211
one hard-edged artificial island
off Jurong, closed to the public,

7
00:00:23,291 --> 00:00:27,125
guarded, and visible only from
a hill, a boat or the air.

8
00:00:27,125 --> 00:00:29,644
What is harder to remember is that

9
00:00:29,724 --> 00:00:32,475
the island is not one island at all.

10
00:00:32,475 --> 00:00:36,105
It is seven, joined by twenty
years of reclamation into

11
00:00:36,185 --> 00:00:39,815
a single chemical fortress
— and before the refineries,

12
00:00:39,895 --> 00:00:43,660
those seven islands held
fishing villages whose headman's

13
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:47,450
account of the strait is
where this story has to start.

14
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:51,279
The channel between the
islands and the Jurong mainland

15
00:00:51,359 --> 00:00:54,700
was called Selat Sembilan
— the Strait of Nine.

16
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:58,518
Ismail bin Mohd Ali, who had
been penghulu, village head,

17
00:00:58,598 --> 00:01:03,236
of Pulau Ayer Merbau and the islands
around it, explained the name by

18
00:01:03,316 --> 00:01:07,612
counting nine islands clustered
there: Ayer Merbau, Ayer Chawan,

19
00:01:07,692 --> 00:01:12,000
Sakra, Bakau, Seraya, Merlimau,
Meskol, Pesek and Mesemut Laut.

20
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,038
His account was recorded
in Berita Harian on the

21
00:01:16,118 --> 00:01:19,899
30th of August 1981,
less than a decade after

22
00:01:19,979 --> 00:01:23,925
he himself had been
relocated to the mainland.

23
00:01:23,925 --> 00:01:27,575
The puzzle is that the
count does not hold.

24
00:01:27,575 --> 00:01:30,862
A British War Office map of 1939 shows

25
00:01:30,942 --> 00:01:34,575
fourteen islands in the strait, not nine.

26
00:01:34,575 --> 00:01:39,006
And the name is older than either
count: on maps of about 1820 the

27
00:01:39,086 --> 00:01:42,901
strait appears as Sinbolan,
Sambulan, Samulun or Semulun,

28
00:01:42,981 --> 00:01:47,549
and as long ago as 1889 the naturalist
H. T. Haughton suggested that

29
00:01:47,629 --> 00:01:52,264
"Semulun" might be the name of an
Orang Laut subgroup — in which case

30
00:01:52,344 --> 00:01:56,650
the Strait of Nine remembers not
a number at all, but a people.

31
00:01:56,650 --> 00:02:00,783
The question is still open, and
it is right to leave it open:

32
00:02:00,863 --> 00:02:03,822
the sea people's own
geography was the first

33
00:02:03,902 --> 00:02:07,425
map of this place, and it
survives mainly in names.

34
00:02:07,425 --> 00:02:12,167
The seven islands that were eventually
merged were Pulau Ayer Chawan,

35
00:02:12,247 --> 00:02:15,452
Pulau Ayer Merbau, Pulau
Merlimau, Pulau Pesek,

36
00:02:15,532 --> 00:02:20,623
Pulau Pesek Kechil — a reef islet also
called Terumbu Pesek — Pulau Sakra,

37
00:02:20,703 --> 00:02:25,525
itself an earlier joining of Sakra
and Pulau Bakau, and Pulau Seraya.

38
00:02:25,525 --> 00:02:29,125
Smaller islets were
absorbed along the way.

39
00:02:29,125 --> 00:02:34,150
Together the seven measured 991 hectares.

40
00:02:34,150 --> 00:02:37,797
Every one of them had been
lived on, most for generations:

41
00:02:37,877 --> 00:02:42,809
Orang Laut groups — Seletar, Selat, and
possibly the Semulun of the old maps —

42
00:02:42,889 --> 00:02:46,665
were there before 1819, the
Orang Seletar building their pau

43
00:02:46,745 --> 00:02:51,677
kajang boats from seraya timber; Bugis
settlers followed, farming and fishing;

44
00:02:51,757 --> 00:02:55,404
and the villages that lasted
into living memory were Malay

45
00:02:55,484 --> 00:02:59,725
fishing kampongs of wooden stilt
houses along palm-fringed shores.

46
00:02:59,725 --> 00:03:03,397
The kampong names are the
map of that lost world,

47
00:03:03,477 --> 00:03:05,850
and they reward reading slowly.

48
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:09,187
Ayer Chawan held Kampong Wak Benchik,

49
00:03:09,267 --> 00:03:11,575
Wak Sekak and Wak Jiling.

50
00:03:11,575 --> 00:03:14,416
Ayer Merbau held Kampong Ayer Merbau,

51
00:03:14,496 --> 00:03:19,075
Kampong Bahru and Kampong Mak
Boak, and a Muslim cemetery.

52
00:03:19,075 --> 00:03:23,729
Merlimau's villages were Tumbok
Opih, Ayer Limau and Limau Manis —

53
00:03:23,809 --> 00:03:28,320
the lime names repeated, because
the island was orchard country;

54
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:31,906
a Teochew settler, Xu
Xifan, came after the Second

55
00:03:31,986 --> 00:03:36,075
World War to grow fruit trees
on a relative's land there.

56
00:03:36,075 --> 00:03:40,025
Seraya, the largest village
island, had four kampongs —

57
00:03:40,105 --> 00:03:43,323
Tengah, Seraya, Pulau
Kecil and Kubang Babi —

58
00:03:43,403 --> 00:03:47,800
a sacred keramat called Keramat
Nenak, and another cemetery.

59
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:53,050
Sakra's village was Tanah Merah;
Pesek's was simply Kampong Pesek.

60
00:03:53,050 --> 00:03:55,725
Some names keep their stories.

61
00:03:55,725 --> 00:03:59,959
"Ayer" means water, and marks the
islands with fresh water on them:

62
00:04:00,039 --> 00:04:03,049
of Ayer Merbau's well,
Ismail the penghulu said,

63
00:04:03,129 --> 00:04:08,135
"Even though it was shallow, the well
never ran dry, and during the dry season,

64
00:04:08,215 --> 00:04:12,400
residents from nearby islands
would come there to collect water."

65
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:16,439
Ayer Chawan's name may remember
the glass cups — chawan —

66
00:04:16,519 --> 00:04:21,000
that Bugis settlers kept digging
up in their pineapple fields.

67
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,982
Pesek's name has two offered explanations,

68
00:04:24,062 --> 00:04:28,722
Bugis holed coins or violin-playing
gatherings — mengesek biola —

69
00:04:28,802 --> 00:04:32,375
and the honest position
is that nobody now knows.

70
00:04:32,375 --> 00:04:37,150
Sakra's may come from kera,
monkeys, or from the shikra bird.

71
00:04:37,150 --> 00:04:40,809
Seraya's last penghulu
was Hamzah Haji Hussein,

72
00:04:40,889 --> 00:04:43,275
whose family came from Bintan.

73
00:04:43,275 --> 00:04:47,965
His voice is kept by the National
Archives' Oral History Centre,

74
00:04:48,045 --> 00:04:51,095
in the series "Communities of Singapore" —

75
00:04:51,175 --> 00:04:55,567
and the collecting is not
finished: his son, Ali bin Hamzah,

76
00:04:55,647 --> 00:04:59,225
sat for an interview on
the 27th of August 2024.

77
00:04:59,225 --> 00:05:02,687
The memory of the islands
is still being gathered,

78
00:05:02,767 --> 00:05:06,875
a generation after the islands
themselves ceased to exist.

79
00:05:06,875 --> 00:05:09,495
Industry did not arrive all at once,

80
00:05:09,575 --> 00:05:12,575
and it did not arrive on an empty shore.

81
00:05:12,575 --> 00:05:16,988
In 1968 the government acquired
the whole of Pulau Ayer

82
00:05:17,068 --> 00:05:21,563
Chawan and leased it to Esso,
which paid for reclamation

83
00:05:21,643 --> 00:05:26,138
that grew the island from
0.73 to 1.54 square kilometres

84
00:05:26,218 --> 00:05:30,875
and built a 200 million
Singapore dollars refinery on it.

85
00:05:30,875 --> 00:05:34,152
The refinery started up in 1970,

86
00:05:34,232 --> 00:05:36,750
at 90,000 barrels a day.

87
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:42,050
The village island had become a
refinery island in about two years.

88
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:44,200
Merlimau followed.

89
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:48,785
The Singapore Petroleum
Company, formed in 1969,

90
00:05:48,865 --> 00:05:53,725
brought its refinery on
stream there in July 1973.

91
00:05:53,725 --> 00:05:59,363
After the first oil shock the small
plant was no longer competitive,

92
00:05:59,443 --> 00:06:03,400
and in 1979 SPC joined
BP and Caltex to form the

93
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:08,525
Singapore Refining Company —
SRC — to upgrade and expand it.

94
00:06:08,525 --> 00:06:13,494
SRC is still there, at 1 Merlimau
Road, now a joint venture of

95
00:06:13,574 --> 00:06:18,055
Chevron and SPC, processing
about 290,000 barrels a day:

96
00:06:18,135 --> 00:06:24,325
the merged island's road names still
carry the old islands inside the fence.

97
00:06:24,325 --> 00:06:28,075
The ambition widened in the 1980s.

98
00:06:28,075 --> 00:06:31,036
The Petrochemical
Corporation of Singapore,

99
00:06:31,116 --> 00:06:35,916
founded in 1977 by the Singapore
Government and a Japanese consortium

100
00:06:35,996 --> 00:06:40,089
led by Sumitomo Chemical, built
its complex on Ayer Merbau,

101
00:06:40,169 --> 00:06:43,695
and in February 1984 brought
Southeast Asia's first

102
00:06:43,775 --> 00:06:48,080
full-fledged ethylene plant on
stream — the step from refining

103
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:52,050
fuel to making the feedstocks
of the chemical industry.

104
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:56,595
On Seraya, merged in 1979 with
the two Seburus islets at a cost

105
00:06:56,675 --> 00:07:02,102
of 32.7 million Singapore dollars and
leased to the public utilities board,

106
00:07:02,182 --> 00:07:05,112
Stage 1 of Pulau Seraya Power Station was

107
00:07:05,192 --> 00:07:09,517
completed in 1988: Singapore's
first offshore power station,

108
00:07:09,597 --> 00:07:14,290
its electricity carried to the
mainland through a submarine cable

109
00:07:14,370 --> 00:07:18,775
tunnel 2.6 kilometres long and
some 20 metres under the sea.

110
00:07:18,775 --> 00:07:22,880
The station, finished in stages by 1997,

111
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:25,575
is now YTL PowerSeraya's.

112
00:07:25,575 --> 00:07:30,023
The islands had, in fact,
already begun to merge,

113
00:07:30,103 --> 00:07:35,199
one industrial plot at a time:
Ayer Merbau absorbed five

114
00:07:35,279 --> 00:07:40,374
small islets in 1976 to 1977,
Seraya took in the Seburus

115
00:07:40,454 --> 00:07:45,075
pair in 1979, Sakra and
Bakau were joined in 1985.

116
00:07:45,075 --> 00:07:48,457
The great amalgamation of the 1990s only

117
00:07:48,537 --> 00:07:51,825
generalised what industry had started.

118
00:07:51,825 --> 00:07:53,700
And the villages?

119
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:57,656
Most islanders were rehoused
in HDB flats on the mainland,

120
00:07:57,736 --> 00:08:02,527
with jobs provided, and the official
history records both sides of it:

121
00:08:02,607 --> 00:08:06,633
an effort to keep communities
together as long as possible,

122
00:08:06,713 --> 00:08:11,375
and residents who spoke of dislocation
and the loss of a community.

123
00:08:11,375 --> 00:08:15,420
One group of islanders was
resettled three times over —

124
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:20,595
from Pulau Samulun to Pulau Merlimau
for the building of Jurong Port,

125
00:08:20,675 --> 00:08:25,250
then to Tanjong Keling, and
finally into HDB flats in Jurong.

126
00:08:25,250 --> 00:08:28,950
No source gives a total
count of the people moved,

127
00:08:29,030 --> 00:08:31,600
and this post will not invent one.

128
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:36,164
What survives is the shape of
it: a whole seascape of villages,

129
00:08:36,244 --> 00:08:38,750
emptied within about a generation.

130
00:08:38,750 --> 00:08:42,924
On the 25th of October
1988, at about half past one

131
00:08:43,004 --> 00:08:47,095
in the afternoon, fire
broke out at an SRC naphtha

132
00:08:47,175 --> 00:08:49,764
storage tank on Pulau Merlimau —

133
00:08:49,844 --> 00:08:55,350
a 120,000-barrel tank whose jammed
floating roof was under repair.

134
00:08:55,350 --> 00:08:59,487
The fire spread to two neighbouring
naphtha tanks and burned

135
00:08:59,567 --> 00:09:03,925
for about five days, until the
morning of the 30th of October.

136
00:09:03,925 --> 00:09:08,725
It remains the worst refinery
fire in Singapore's history.

137
00:09:08,725 --> 00:09:12,434
No lives were lost; twenty-five
people were injured,

138
00:09:12,514 --> 00:09:15,575
most of them firefighters, five seriously.

139
00:09:15,575 --> 00:09:20,025
It was SRC's second
major fire in four years.

140
00:09:20,025 --> 00:09:24,915
The big refinery fire many people
remember, in September 2011,

141
00:09:24,995 --> 00:09:29,645
was at Pulau Bukom, not here
— but Merlimau's five-day burn

142
00:09:29,725 --> 00:09:33,413
is the one that belongs
to this island's story,

143
00:09:33,493 --> 00:09:39,425
and Jurong Island's first fire station
followed in 1998, a second in 2008.

144
00:09:39,425 --> 00:09:44,925
The plan to amalgamate the seven
islands was conceived in the 1980s;

145
00:09:45,005 --> 00:09:48,290
JTC became the responsible agent in 1991,

146
00:09:48,370 --> 00:09:53,214
and industry was signing before
the island existed — DuPont,

147
00:09:53,294 --> 00:09:58,300
Jurong Island's first lessee,
took its plot on Sakra in 1990.

148
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:02,275
Reclamation began in 1995.

149
00:10:02,275 --> 00:10:06,650
Japan's Penta-Ocean Construction
led the joint venture

150
00:10:06,730 --> 00:10:11,022
that won the Phase 3 contract
— 1.3 billion Singapore

151
00:10:11,102 --> 00:10:15,065
dollars to reclaim 977
hectares, then the largest

152
00:10:15,145 --> 00:10:19,600
single reclamation contract
ever awarded in Singapore.

153
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:23,576
The work finished on the
25th of September 2009,

154
00:10:23,656 --> 00:10:26,275
twenty years ahead of schedule.

155
00:10:26,275 --> 00:10:29,817
The seven islands' 991 hectares had

156
00:10:29,897 --> 00:10:33,438
become 3,000 hectares of new land —

157
00:10:33,518 --> 00:10:38,175
about 3,200 by 2015,
after smaller additions.

158
00:10:38,175 --> 00:10:42,064
The road link came first:
the Jurong Island Highway

159
00:10:42,144 --> 00:10:45,488
dates from March 1999,
and the road link was

160
00:10:45,568 --> 00:10:49,225
officially opened on the
10th of February 2000.

161
00:10:49,225 --> 00:10:54,258
On the 14th of October 2000, Prime
Minister Goh Chok Tong officially

162
00:10:54,338 --> 00:10:58,925
opened Jurong Island and called
it a "Herculean achievement".

163
00:10:58,925 --> 00:11:02,701
By opening day, more than
sixty companies and over 20

164
00:11:02,781 --> 00:11:06,557
billion Singapore dollars
in investments were already

165
00:11:06,637 --> 00:11:10,275
on the island, and over
6,000 people worked there.

166
00:11:10,275 --> 00:11:13,095
The United States Geological Survey's

167
00:11:13,175 --> 00:11:16,075
Landsat satellites watched it happen.

168
00:11:16,075 --> 00:11:19,514
Their frames, all public
domain, are the only honest

169
00:11:19,594 --> 00:11:22,966
before-and-after this post
can offer — there are no

170
00:11:23,046 --> 00:11:27,501
copyright-clear photographs of the
kampong era, and the reclamation

171
00:11:27,581 --> 00:11:31,100
itself was photographed
mostly for official records.

172
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:34,750
What the satellites saw
needs no commentary:

173
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:38,900
The island the satellites
see is only the top layer.

174
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:43,247
Beneath Banyan Basin, 130
metres under the seabed,

175
00:11:43,327 --> 00:11:46,789
five rock caverns — each 27 metres high,

176
00:11:46,869 --> 00:11:52,102
20 metres wide and 340 metres
long — hold 1.47 million cubic

177
00:11:52,182 --> 00:11:56,875
metres of crude oil,
condensate, naphtha and gas oil.

178
00:11:56,875 --> 00:12:02,394
The Jurong Rock Caverns were officially
opened on the 2nd of September 2014:

179
00:12:02,474 --> 00:12:06,226
storage space Singapore did
not have on the surface,

180
00:12:06,306 --> 00:12:10,800
dug out of the rock below the
island it had built on the sea.

181
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:14,168
And on a 40-hectare plot
at the island's southern

182
00:12:14,248 --> 00:12:17,264
tip stands the reason
readers of this blog's

183
00:12:17,344 --> 00:12:20,300
pipelines story will know the island best.

184
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:24,477
Until 2013, every molecule
of Singapore's natural

185
00:12:24,557 --> 00:12:29,075
gas arrived by pipeline
from Malaysia and Indonesia.

186
00:12:29,075 --> 00:12:33,928
The Singapore LNG terminal —
decided in 2006, built for 1.7

187
00:12:34,008 --> 00:12:39,445
billion Singapore dollars — began
commercial operations on the 7th

188
00:12:39,525 --> 00:12:44,625
of May 2013 and was officially
opened the following February.

189
00:12:44,625 --> 00:12:49,188
It is the insurance policy made
concrete: a second way in for the

190
00:12:49,268 --> 00:12:53,545
fuel that makes about 95 per
cent of Singapore's electricity,

191
00:12:53,625 --> 00:12:58,625
standing on an island that did not
exist when the pipelines were laid.

192
00:12:58,625 --> 00:13:03,415
In 2001 Jurong Island was
gazetted a protected area,

193
00:13:03,495 --> 00:13:05,275
and it remains one.

194
00:13:05,275 --> 00:13:10,107
Entry requires a Jurong Island Pass,
and a pass requires a sponsor —

195
00:13:10,187 --> 00:13:13,791
a company on the island
must vouch for the visitor;

196
00:13:13,871 --> 00:13:18,350
there is no applying as an
individual, no tour, no public day.

197
00:13:18,350 --> 00:13:22,207
Visitors enrol their biometrics
and clear a checkpoint;

198
00:13:22,287 --> 00:13:28,721
the Army guards the land, the Police Coast
Guard patrols the surrounding restricted waters,

199
00:13:28,801 --> 00:13:31,450
and photography inside is prohibited.

200
00:13:31,450 --> 00:13:36,066
More than a hundred energy and
chemical companies now operate there,

201
00:13:36,146 --> 00:13:39,934
linked by over a hundred
kilometres of shared pipelines,

202
00:13:40,014 --> 00:13:42,500
one plant's output feeding the next.

203
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:47,019
So every photograph in this post
was taken from outside the fence —

204
00:13:47,099 --> 00:13:50,875
from Jurong Hill, from the
mainland shore, from a boat.

205
00:13:50,875 --> 00:13:54,545
That is not a limitation
the post can work around;

206
00:13:54,625 --> 00:13:56,575
it is part of the subject.

207
00:13:56,575 --> 00:14:00,038
Jurong Island is the
most consequential place

208
00:14:00,118 --> 00:14:03,425
in Singapore that Singaporeans cannot see.

209
00:14:03,425 --> 00:14:07,825
The island turned twenty-five
in 2025, and the anniversary

210
00:14:07,905 --> 00:14:12,925
brought a change of direction
rather than a celebration of scale.

211
00:14:12,925 --> 00:14:17,578
At the Jurong Island 25th
Anniversary Dinner in November 2025,

212
00:14:17,658 --> 00:14:21,319
the refreshed plan was
set out: a premier hub for

213
00:14:21,399 --> 00:14:25,824
specialty chemicals and
sustainable materials, and a global

214
00:14:25,904 --> 00:14:29,950
testbed for new energies
and low-carbon technologies.

215
00:14:29,950 --> 00:14:33,120
Output of sustainable products

216
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:36,125
is up 1.4 times since 2019.

217
00:14:36,125 --> 00:14:40,230
On the island now: a bench-scale
rig by IHI and A*STAR making

218
00:14:40,310 --> 00:14:44,277
sustainable aviation fuel from
hydrogen and carbon dioxide;

219
00:14:44,357 --> 00:14:48,393
a consortium exploring low- or
zero-carbon ammonia for power

220
00:14:48,473 --> 00:14:52,441
generation and bunkering; the
island's largest solar array,

221
00:14:52,521 --> 00:14:56,763
about 120 megawatts-peak, from
Sembcorp; a 62 million Singapore

222
00:14:56,843 --> 00:15:00,467
dollars low-carbon technology
testbed opening in 2027;

223
00:15:00,547 --> 00:15:05,075
and about 20 hectares set aside
for a low-carbon data centre park.

224
00:15:05,075 --> 00:15:08,193
The island built for
oil is being retooled,

225
00:15:08,273 --> 00:15:10,950
carefully, for what comes after oil.

226
00:15:10,950 --> 00:15:14,470
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore has made

227
00:15:14,550 --> 00:15:17,934
land before — but Jurong
Island is the only time it

228
00:15:18,014 --> 00:15:21,942
made land out of other inhabited
islands, villages and all,

229
00:15:22,022 --> 00:15:25,350
and then closed the result
to the public forever.

230
00:15:25,350 --> 00:15:29,749
The seven islands are gone from
the map and present everywhere in

231
00:15:29,829 --> 00:15:34,091
the island that replaced them:
in 1 Merlimau Road, in Sakra and

232
00:15:34,171 --> 00:15:38,650
Seraya and Banyan, in a strait
that is no longer called Sembilan.

233
00:15:38,650 --> 00:15:43,358
The penghulu's nine islands were
really fourteen, and are now one —

234
00:15:43,438 --> 00:15:47,225
and the one is an island
almost nobody will ever see.
